course: dictionary
lesson: 2319
title: Crossly, annoyed but not furious
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Crossly is an adverb. It tells us how someone speaks, looks or acts, and the -ly ending is a clue. «"Shoes on, now!" she said crossly.» The word crossly tells you she was annoyed, but the sentence does not say she was roaring with rage.
explain: Context shows how strong the feeling is. A grown-up telling a child off is usually a bit cross, not furious. Ask: what word would fit here? In «She said crossly», a word like happily or sleepily would not fit the telling-off, so crossly must mean in an annoyed way.
explain: The Mole answers «rather pettishly» (a bit crossly) «Oh, it's all very well to talk», because he is new to the river and cannot see how to get across. The brackets tell you pettishly means a bit crossly, and the words he says show he is fed up.
fact: Crossly means in an angry or annoyed way.
fact: Crossly is an adverb: it tells us how someone speaks, looks or acts.
fact: Crossly shows annoyance, not fury: it is milder than shouting or roaring.
fact: Phrases to know: «said crossly», «looked crossly», «answered crossly».
fact: «Pettishly» is an older word that means a bit crossly, as the Mole answers in the old story.
exercise: «"Shoes on, now!" she said crossly.» How did she say it? | Quietly, in a whisper | Happily, with a big smile | *In an angry or annoyed way | Sleepily, with a yawn
exercise: Which phrase shows someone speaking in an annoyed way? | slept soundly | laughed happily | *answered crossly | sang softly
exercise: Find and copy the adverb in: «He shut the door crossly and sat down.» | door | down | sat | *crossly
test: Which word means in an angry or annoyed way? | sleepily | *crossly | happily | quietly
test: Which description fits crossly best? | a happy laugh | a long, sad sob | *a bit annoyed | a loud roar of fury
exam: «"Do not touch that," Mum said ___.» | sleepily | sadly | happily | *crossly
